import{_ as n,C as s,o as r,c as o,j as t,a as i,E as h,a6 as l}from"./chunks/framework.CCKCzdWs.js";const m=JSON.parse(`{"title":"Results in Zephyr Scale","description":"Send a finished CI run's results to Zephyr Scale Cloud as one test run, one result per test case key, with a dry run first and a link back to the Piwi run.","frontmatter":{"title":"Results in Zephyr Scale","description":"Send a finished CI run's results to Zephyr Scale Cloud as one test run, one result per test case key, with a dry run first and a link back to the Piwi run.","lang":"en-US","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://piwitests.dev/features/test-management.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:url","content":"https://piwitests.dev/features/test-management.html"}],["meta",{"property":"og:title","content":"Results in Zephyr Scale | Piwi for Playwright"}],["meta",{"property":"og:description","content":"Send a finished CI run's results to Zephyr Scale Cloud as one test run, one result per test case key, with a dry run first and a link back to the Piwi run."}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"features/test-management.md","filePath":"features/test-management.md","lastUpdated":1794516094000}`),d={name:"features/test-management.md"};function u(c,e,p,y,g,f){const a=s("Needs");return r(),o("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"results-in-zephyr-scale",tabindex:"-1"},[i("Results in Zephyr Scale "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#results-in-zephyr-scale","aria-label":'Permalink to "Results in Zephyr Scale"'},"​")],-1)),h(a,{reporter:"",admin:""}),e[1]||(e[1]=l('<p>When a CI run of the whole suite finishes, Piwi can write its results to Zephyr Scale Cloud: one <strong>test run</strong> (a test cycle in Zephyr) holding one result for each test case key the run&#39;s tests carry. The test run links back to the run&#39;s page in Piwi, and the run&#39;s page shows what was sent.</p><p>It is <strong>off until an <a href="/operate/zephyr-scale.html#bind-a-project">administrator connects Zephyr Scale and binds the project</a></strong>, and a new binding starts as a <strong>dry run</strong> that sends nothing.</p><h2 id="when-a-run-is-sent" tabindex="-1">When a run is sent <a class="header-anchor" href="#when-a-run-is-sent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;When a run is sent&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A run is sent once, when it finishes, if all of these hold:</p><ul><li>the project is bound to a Zephyr project, the binding is <strong>Enabled</strong>, and the run is in the binding&#39;s branches and environments;</li><li>the run came from CI and ran the whole suite to the end. A partial run (a filter, a selection, a rerun of the failed tests), a run that was interrupted or timed out, a run from a developer&#39;s machine, the desktop app or an editor, a lab run and an imported run send nothing;</li><li>the project&#39;s test case catalog has been read, and at least one test of the run carries a key the catalog holds.</li></ul><p>A run split across shards is sent once, after its last shard reports, with the results of every shard. Sending is a background action, so the run is not held up. Only two failures are retried on their own, with a growing wait: Zephyr asking Piwi to slow down (a 429), and a failure after Zephyr named the test run, which repeats only the step that failed. An upload that gets no answer is <strong>Outcome unknown</strong> and waits for <strong>Sync again</strong> (see below).</p><p>The binding is read again when the results go out. If it was turned off, became a dry run or names another Zephyr project since the run finished, nothing is sent and the run&#39;s panel says why. If Zephyr already holds the test run by then, the send fails and names it, so you can find it in Zephyr.</p><h2 id="what-is-sent" tabindex="-1">What is sent <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-sent" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What is sent&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>One result per test case key.</strong> When several tests carry the same key, or one test runs in several browsers or repeats, the worst outcome wins: a failure beats a flaky test, which beats a pass.</li><li><strong>Only keys the catalog holds.</strong> A key a test carries that is not a case in the project is left out and listed on the run&#39;s page, so a typo never creates anything in Zephyr. Tests with no key are not sent.</li><li><strong>Flaky and skipped tests follow the binding.</strong> A flaky test is reported as a pass, as a fail, or with the flaky status you name; a skipped test is left out or reported as not executed. See <strong>Result statuses</strong> under <a href="/operate/zephyr-scale.html#bind-a-project">Bind a project</a>.</li><li><strong>The first line of the error</strong> is added to a failed result only when the binding says so. It is off by default.</li><li><strong>The test run&#39;s name</strong> comes from the binding&#39;s <a href="/operate/zephyr-scale.html#the-test-run-name">name template</a>. Its description holds the run&#39;s address in Piwi, the branch, the short commit and the counts sent.</li></ul><p>Piwi never imports test cases, steps or results from Zephyr: the catalog it keeps holds only each case&#39;s key, name, status and folder.</p><h2 id="on-the-run-s-page" tabindex="-1">On the run&#39;s page <a class="header-anchor" href="#on-the-run-s-page" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;On the run&#39;s page&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A run that was sent, or would be, shows a <strong>Zephyr Scale</strong> panel in its header:</p><ul><li><strong>A live send</strong> shows the key of the test run, how many results were sent and the counts, for example <code>SHOP-R2</code>, &quot;12 results sent: 10 passed, 1 failed, 1 flaky, 0 not executed&quot;. The key is also listed with the run&#39;s links.</li><li><strong>A dry run</strong> says that nothing was sent and shows how many results a live sync would send, with their counts, and the keys left out because the catalog does not hold them, so you can check them before you turn the dry run off.</li><li><strong>A run that was not sent</strong> says why: for instance that the catalog was not read yet or that no test carries a mapped key.</li><li><strong>A send that waits to retry</strong> says that sending failed, at what time the next attempt is due, and the error.</li><li><strong>A failed send</strong> shows the error, and Maintainers and above get <strong>Sync again</strong>.</li></ul><p>The panel keeps itself current: it appears within about a minute of the run ending, follows a send while it runs, and reads a retry again once it is due.</p><h2 id="outcome-unknown-and-sync-again" tabindex="-1">Outcome unknown, and Sync again <a class="header-anchor" href="#outcome-unknown-and-sync-again" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Outcome unknown, and Sync again&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Zephyr creates a new test run every time results are uploaded, so Piwi never uploads the same run blindly. It records that an upload started before it begins, and the test run&#39;s key as soon as Zephyr names it.</p><p>A large upload can take longer than the request is allowed to wait, so Piwi may not know whether Zephyr received it. The panel then says the outcome is unknown. It does not retry on its own. <strong>Sync again</strong> first looks in Zephyr for the test run that run&#39;s upload may have made, by its name. It reads the test runs that hold the latest results of one of the run&#39;s test cases: those that ended since the upload began, then, when none of them is the one, all of them. Whether Zephyr gives uploaded results an end date is unchecked, so the search does not depend on it:</p><ul><li>one that is found is linked to the Piwi run and is not made again;</li><li>none found, after every test run was read, uploads the results again;</li><li>when Zephyr gives no answer Piwi can trust (more executions or test runs than it reads, or a test run it cannot read), nothing is sent, the send stays failed and the message says why. Check the project in Zephyr before you try again.</li></ul><p>Two people who press <strong>Sync again</strong> at the same time send the results once: the second is told the sync changed.</p><p>A send that failed before the upload, or after the test run was made, only repeats the step that failed. <strong>Sync again</strong> works on a failed send; a dry run has nothing to repeat. It is refused, and says why, while the binding is turned off, is a dry run, or names another connection or another Zephyr project.</p><h2 id="linked-back-to-the-run" tabindex="-1">Linked back to the run <a class="header-anchor" href="#linked-back-to-the-run" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Linked back to the run&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The test run in Zephyr gets a web link to the Piwi run&#39;s page, titled with the run&#39;s number, when the server knows its public address: set <a href="/reference/configuration.html#integrations"><code>PIWI_SITE_URL</code></a>. The Piwi run lists the Zephyr test run among its links, as the key, and removing the link there does not delete anything in Zephyr.</p><h2 id="good-to-know" tabindex="-1">Good to know <a class="header-anchor" href="#good-to-know" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Good to know&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li>Zephyr Scale <strong>Cloud</strong> only. Zephyr Scale Server and Data Center, Xray and other tools are not supported.</li><li>Piwi writes through the connection&#39;s token, one request budget per connection, shared with the catalog. See <a href="/operate/zephyr-scale.html#request-rate">Request rate</a>.</li><li>If you would rather have Zephyr own the test run, Playwright&#39;s built-in <code>junit</code> reporter writes a file Zephyr Scale can import itself. Use one or the other for a project, not both, or the results are recorded twice.</li></ul><h2 id="related" tabindex="-1">Related <a class="header-anchor" href="#related" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Related&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><a href="/operate/zephyr-scale.html">Zephyr Scale Cloud</a>: connect it, bind a project and read the test case catalog</li><li><a href="/reference/test-metadata.html">Test metadata</a>: the tags and external IDs a test carries its case key in</li><li><a href="./issue-tracking.html">Issue tracking (Jira)</a>: the other integration that writes to an external tool</li><li><a href="/operate/integrations.html">Integrations</a>: how connections are managed</li></ul>',26))])}const k=n(d,[["render",u]]);export{m as __pageData,k as default};
